ion controls, not additional natural datasets or isolated component ablations.\\\",\n    \\\"ablation_studies is empty and its evidence directory is missing. Existing search variants may support a clearly separated exploratory supplement, but cannot establish the causal contribution of nonlinear fitting, risk selection, or studentization.\\\",\n    \\\"The inspected ICLR template and paper.sty specify a single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height. The template's named author and final-copy switch must not survive into an anonymized submission. Historical style-header text does not establish official ICLR 2026 template provenance.\\\",\n    \\\"references.bib and references_provenance.json are now present, but a key-only scan exposed unscreened recent retrieval candidates outside the guarded exact-work plan. Do not read or cite those candidates without safe screening; no associated abstracts or pages were read here, and no prohibited work was identified. Bibliography availability is not evidence of permissible or actually consulted literature.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Label-only mean intervals\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The matched reference uses exact binomial Clopper-Pearson intervals for binary outcomes and Student-t intervals for real outcomes. It isolates whether a frozen score adds practical value; Student-t is not distribution-free exact for the empirical real source.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"PPI and PPI++ mean intervals\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Both were evaluated on identical inputs using ppi-python 0.2.3, with coefficient 1 and its optimized coefficient respectively. Keep them distinct: the original comparator remains scientifically relevant, and the optimized reference is essential to reveal the six conditions without a width benefit. Their asymptotic status and rare-event behavior must accompany width comparisons.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Risk-adaptive cross-fitted frozen-score controls\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"This is the frozen selection with final evidence: nested training-only zero/affine/spline calibration, finite-unlabeled-sample multiplier, weighted held-out residuals, and a shared-pool variance computed after combining controls. Report its t-based return path and label-only fallbacks, not its separate unreturned certificate.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Constant-score and permuted-score perturbations\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"They reuse the nonlinear records under specified score transformations and test dependence on score information without changing the method. Present alongside the independently uninformative condition; do not call them isolated ablations of calibration or variance estimation.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n    \\\"Primary: all seven final conditions, each with the frozen method and all three matched comparators; foreground paired width differences or ratios of mean widths together with per-method coverage uncertainty. Never select only the favorable real source.\\\",\n    \\\"Gene-expression empirical source: mean widths are 0.4488074070562893 for the frozen method, 0.4759006085515874 for PPI++, 0.4727476605089987 for PPI, and 1.5263806745747615 for label-only inference. Candidate coverage is 0.9575, with stored simultaneous interval [0.9163118126035984, 0.9824159148164467]. This is resampling from the frozen empirical source, not external biological-population validation.\\\",\n    \\\"Boundary of benefit: every other condition has a positive paired mean-width difference versus PPI++; the nonlinear condition itself does not show a benefit. Distinguish improvement relative to label-only or unoptimized PPI from improvement relative to PPI++.\\\",\n    \\\"Coverage: show the 0.95 target and stored simultaneous Clopper-Pearson intervals across seven conditions and four methods. The frozen method's observed condition coverage spans 0.95 to 0.98, but its lower uncertainty bounds do not establish coverage at or above 0.95.\\\",\n    \\\"Rare outcomes: PPI++ observed coverage is 0.92 and its stored interval is [0.868958106205729, 0.9562534828761202], which still includes 0.95. Five of its 400 intervals have zero width. Retain its width evidence with these caveats rather than treating narrower intervals as automatically valid or declaring a statistically established coverage failure.\\\",\n    \\\"Metric audit: the reported aggregate width ratio averages capped per-episode ratios and assigns 100 to a positive candidate width over a zero-width reference. Use paired absolute differences and ratios of means for scientific interpretation; distinguish these estimands explicitly and retain the original aggregate only as a documented secondary result.\\\",\n    \\\"Mechanistic scope: deterministic-control finite-N variance/headroom identities and fold-offset invariance are explanatory algebra. Overlapping fitted folds, heuristic family selection, critical-value differences, clipping, and fallbacks prevent attributing the measured width change to one component without unavailable controlled ablations.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n    \\\"Figure 1, early overview: fixed inputs, training-only control selection, held-out correction, shared-unlabeled aggregation, and the distinction between the returned interval and empirical evaluation.\\\",\n    \\\"Figure 2, primary efficiency comparison: two vertically stacked forest-plot panels covering all seven conditions, with three comparator series showing the frozen method's ratio of mean widths and stored paired-bootstrap uncertainty.\\\",\n    \\\"Figure 3, coverage boundary: the same condition partition, all four methods, simultaneous coverage intervals, and a single nominal 0.95 reference line; retain rare-event behavior in the main text.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"fixed_resource_overview\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the fixed-resource mean-inference object and training boundary legible before quantitative results; distinguish an evaluated asymptotic return path from a coverage certificate.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"information_boundary\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"not_applicable_conceptual\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"fit_and_combine\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use the inspected single-column 5.5-inch text width, not a hypothetical two-column span. Two compact conceptual panels at 5.5 by about 2.0 inches leave room for 9-10pt text; put the overview on page 1 or 2, with a footprint near one quarter page including caption. Panel one has fixed inputs and outer-training-only selection; panel two has held-out residuals plus shared-pool control aggregation and the returned interval. Use no more than six labeled functional groups per panel and no invented architecture or measured improvement arrows.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"paired_width_comparison\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Show where the frozen method is narrower or wider against each reference without conflating mean ratios, ratios of means, or zero-denominator penalties.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"forest_plot\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"condition\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"mean_candidate_width_divided_by_mean_comparator_width\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"predictive_conditions_and_information_controls\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Two vertically stacked panels at 5.5 inches wide, approximately 3.3-3.7 inches tall in total, use a common log-ratio axis and reference at one. Panel one contains gene expression, linear, nonlinear, and rare outcomes; panel two contains uninformative, constant-score, and permuted-score conditions. Three offset point/interval series identify label-only, PPI, and PPI++ comparators. Stacking avoids illegible labels in half-width panels. Reserve about half a page including caption; minimum rendered text 8pt, preferably 9pt. State that a width advantage alone is not a coverage advantage.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 4,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"coverage_with_uncertainty\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Keep uncertainty about nominal coverage visible for every method and condition, including the apparent rare-event width advantage of PPI++.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"forest_plot\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"condition\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"empirical_coverage_with_simultaneous_binomial_interval\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"predictive_conditions_and_information_controls\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Match Figure 2's two-panel vertical condition partition and full 5.5-inch single-column width. Four offset series show the frozen method and three references; use a shared coverage axis covering all stored interval endpoints and a single 0.95 line. Approximately 3.3-3.7 inches total height and about half a page with caption preserve 8-9pt labels. Do not crop lower bounds, add thresholded success colors, or convert an interval spanning 0.95 into a pass/fail claim.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 4,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Provide derivation, implementation, provenance, and endpoint-level audit depth after the references without moving the mixed width result, coverage uncertainty, or rare-event caveat out of the main paper; include only already available artifacts or deterministic analyses of them.\\\",\n    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Extended control construction and variance identities\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Expand the inspected selected src/METHOD.md and src/program.py into deterministic-control finite-N variance, oracle affine/nonlinear headroom, and fold-offset cancellation derivations. Include the weighted shared-pool covariance terms, nested family-selection details, signed multipliers, spline penalties, t critical value, clipping, and fallback rules. State assumptions separately for deterministic controls and learned cross-fit asymptotics. Do not claim the sufficient stability assumptions were verified empirically, and do not report unexecuted test_controls.py as a passed test.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"result.best_correct_score\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete paired interval and coverage tables\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Tabulate all seven conditions and all four methods from final.evidence.episodes, including mean widths, coverage counts, stored coverage intervals, paired width-difference intervals, ratio-of-mean intervals, and missingness. Document multiplicity and finite bootstrap resolution. Keep the principal comparisons in the main text; these tables expose exact values rather than adding new experimental conditions.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.nominal_coverage\\\", \\\"final.metric.failure_fraction\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Rare outcomes and width-ratio definitions\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Audit the five zero-width PPI++ episodes, using endpoint equality and seed identifiers; compare absolute mean differences, ratios of means, and the evaluator's capped per-episode ratios. Explain the 100 cap and zero-denominator convention from task/evaluate_final.py. Include the available baseline coverage interval and warn that the rare-outcome baseline's point estimate below nominal does not itself establish undercoverage. No uncapped infinite episode-ratio average is needed.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.mean_width_ratio\\\", \\\"final.metric.empirical_coverage\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Frozen-source provenance and reproduction\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Use task/resources/final/manifest.json, task/evaluate_final.py, task/requirements.txt, the selected source tree, and final manifests to document 128 labels, 8192 unlabeled scores, final seeds 1000-1399, pairing, normalized-record search/final separation, and empirical-source sampling with replacement. Describe duplicate-group splitting without claiming promoter-sequence independence. Supply executable extraction/plot commands when implemented, pin actual dependencies, and map each final figure/table to its source-producing script. Do not invent unexecuted runtimes or a missing synthetic-data producer; frozen arrays are available for replay. Record unresolved independent NPZ redistribution rights and avoid bundling restricted data. Local paths are private provenance, not identifying links for the manuscript.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"result.best_correct_score\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Exploratory configuration comparisons and selection boundary\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Only if useful to explain selection, summarize the existing 100-repeat search evidence separately for pooled-residual controls, offset-invariant risk-adaptive controls, and smooth covariance stacking. Use source identities, not chronology, and explicitly label these as adaptively compared search configurations rather than final-replicate ablations. Do not pool their seeds with the 400 final repeats, infer unknown selection frequencies, or present an unavailable original-incumbent performance comparison. No study.* sources exist, so do not fabricate downstream-study selection counts or score differences. Omit this subsection if it adds no scientific value.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"result.top_program_01\\\", \\\"result.top_program_02\\\", \\\"result.top_program_03\\\", \\\"result.best_correct_score\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Disclose the documented LLM roles in ideation, source editing, and paper framing, then add the writer's actual drafting role. Separate externally executed evaluation evidence from generated prose and algebra. Keep the disclosure anonymous; do not assert human verification, executed tests, or literature consultation absent a record.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"result.best_correct_score\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n    \\\"Search Progression\\\",\n    \\\"Campaign Phases\\\",\n    \\\"Milestones\\\",\n    \\\"First Valid Result\\\",\n    \\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\",\n    \\\"Iteration History\\\",\n    \\\"From Baseline to Winner\\\",\n    \\\"Universal Interval Shortening\\\",\n    \\\"Certified Coverage of the Returned Method\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n    \\\"we then tried\\\",\n    \\\"first valid result\\\",\n    \\\"subsequent improvements\\\",\n    \\\"next-best\\\",\n    \\\"winning iteration\\\",\n    \\\"state of the art\\\",\n    \\\"uniformly narrower\\\",\n    \\\"guaranteed nominal coverage for the returned intervals\\\",\n    \\\"the nonlinear component caused the observed gain\\\",\n    \\\"correctness rate\\\",\n    \\\"correct and eligible\\\",\n    \\\"eligible\\\",\n    \\\"combined_score\\\",\n    \\\"rubric\\\",\n    \\\"worker_01\\\",\n    \\\"worker_02\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n    \\\"PRIVATE-ONLY AUDIT: ground the actual final completed/qualified/true outcome in final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome, and final.qualification.correct. There is no failed final qualification to disclose in this run. If later authoritative artifacts change it to not_qualified, retain the failure prominently and in review/audit/revision; missing gate causes remain unknown. Neither publication eligibility nor operational acceptance proves scientific validity.\\\",\n    \\\"PRIVATE-ONLY AUDIT: task/resources/final/manifest.json and task/evaluate_final.py explicitly fix scientific_validity to conservative zero because arbitrary theoretical assumptions cannot be certified. Do not reinterpret this flag as a diagnosed coverage, adaptivity, or integrity defect. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; prefer raw evaluator-observed endpoints and evaluator-produced summaries to candidate self-descriptions.\\\",\n    \\\"Use a shared-thesis, condition-specific organization, not separate dataset papers or numbered experiments. Suggested visible sections: Introduction; Related Work; Mean Inference with Frozen Scores; Control Construction and Validity Boundaries; Evaluation Design; Conditional Width Gains and Coverage; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion.\\\",\n    \\\"Target nine substantive body pages in the inspected single-column ICLR geometry. Planning allocations including figures: title/abstract 0.5 pages; introduction and early overview 1.2; related work 0.6; method 1.6; identities and validity boundaries 1.1; evaluation design 0.7; results 2.1; discussion/limitations 0.9; conclusion 0.3. These are layout budgets, not measured results. If the body finishes at eight pages, its last page must be at least 75% full. Spend slack on clearer assumptions, comparator differences, uncertainty definitions, and failure analysis, not filler or new unrun experiments.\\\",\n    \\\"The format has one text column: column width and full text width are both 5.5 inches. All three required figures are planned at full text width, never as tiny half-width two-column figures. Use a compact full-width evaluation-design table for conditions, targets, pairing, and score transformations. Explain differing Student-t/normal critical values and fallbacks as possible comparison contributors, not isolated explanations.\\\",\n    \\\"The supplied .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex uses paper.sty; inspect official ICLR 2026 formatting provenance before final submission using venue-only sources. Do not claim that this stage verified the style as the latest official release. Remove named authors, affiliations, acknowledgments, and funding; use review mode rather than the template's named final-copy author. Preserve the orchestrator-owned watermark block.\\\",\n    \\\"Keep the constant-score and permuted-score results, all six non-benefit comparisons versus PPI++, and rare-outcome baseline caveats in the main body. The optional search supplement must not displace evidence essential to the thesis. Authoritative later appendix-ranked analyses belong after references without retroactively modifying this strategy.\\\",\n    \\\"No component ablation, parameter sweep, oracle simulation, selection-frequency study, extra real dataset, or predictor retraining was completed in the available final evidence. Do not schedule them as required figures or write them as results. The searched configurations are not clean causal ablations.\\\",\n    \\\"Resource fixing is within each matched replicate. Repeated sampling changes the labeled and unlabeled draws; coverage is not conditional on every observed input being fixed. For the real source the target is its normalized empirical-source mean, not an external biological population. Only scalar predictions/outcomes are observed; no new backbone, promoter-feature, or sequence-disjointness claim is supported.\\\",\n    \\\"Explain the estimator as a weighted held-out residual mean plus a shared-unlabeled control mean. Explain offset invariance without claiming pooled residual studentization empirically failed. The deterministic variance identity does not become an exact conditional variance formula for overlapping learned folds. The inner-fold excess-risk uncertainty is a selection heuristic, not a calibrated hypothesis test.\\\",\n    \\\"The internal bounded certificate is computed but not returned. The reported interval uses Student-t critical values and selected fallbacks. A Clopper-Pearson fallback alone does not certify a data-dependent mixture, and an unbiased deterministic variance estimate alone does not imply exact t coverage.\\\",\n    \\\"Use the stored summaries at final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes[0].solution.summary, and raw paired endpoints in each episode's solution.cases. Most other episodes do not repeat the summary. Required quantitative figures must be reproducible deterministic views of these artifacts, not new simulations.\\\",\n    \\\"Coverage uncertainty uses Clopper-Pearson with a joint 95% Bonferroni family over seven conditions and four methods. Width-comparison intervals are paired percentile bootstrap summaries from 512 resamples with recorded multiplicity adjustment and seed 79231. Describe their finite-bootstrap tail resolution and do not call bootstrap simultaneous coverage exact. Preserve pairing across methods and related score perturbations.\\\",\n    \\\"A ratio of mean widths differs from the mean of episode ratios. Derive plotted ratio points from stored mean widths and use the matching stored ratio intervals; retain the calculation and final.evidence.episodes as provenance. Do not drop zero-width observations or use the capped aggregate as a scientific width advantage. Derived endpoint counts and aggregates must be explicitly sourced, not described as newly executed experiments.\\\",\n    \\\"Reference scope is restricted. Existing exact permitted PPI and Cross-PPI records are starting points, not proof that their full texts were consulted. Resolve PPI++ only by the permitted arXiv:2311.01453 record and other permitted starter works only as needed. Do not browse cited-by lists, related-paper feeds, or unscreened recent candidates in the current bibliography. If prohibited content is actually encountered, stop immediately and record location and extent without trying to identify the unnamed paper. Do not invent missing references or novelty claims.\\\",\n    \\\"Use the generated overview workflow only once, for fixed_resource_overview; the orchestrator owns its execution. Honor the no-paid-API constraint: request deterministic draw.io rendering without optional paid image restyling, and do not invoke a paid endpoint. If the orchestration cannot honor that constraint, document the blocker rather than silently paying or changing the figure's recorded ownership.\\\",\n    \\\"For quantitative figures retain plotting source and export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG; LaTeX must use the PDF whenever it exists. Preserve editable draw.io source for the overview and use its vector export when available. Raster-only figures must be at least 2400 pixels wide at full-width placement or 1200 pixels at column-width placement; regenerate at source resolution rather than upscale. At 5.5 inches, a 300-DPI-only raster export is insufficient for the full-width minimum, so export a larger raster too if needed.\\\",\n    \\\"After references use clearpage, appendix, and a Supplementary Information section with the grounded subsections above and a separate LLM Usage subsection. References and supplement do not fill the body-page requirement. Do not invent an ICLR checklist if no official requirement is established; comply with actual venue-required forms.\\\",\n    \\\"This stage creates strategy artifacts only. Do not write paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, generate final figures, invoke concept rendering, or run final-paper validation here. Later writing must maintain qualification disclosure and scope through review, audit, and revision.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n}\nEOF\ncat > paper_strategy.md <<'EOF'\n# Paper Strategy: Conditional Gains and Limits of Frozen-Score Controls\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status:** ready. **Family:** \"'`paper_standalone`, using `write-paper-standalone`.\n\n**Provisional title:** Risk-Adaptive Frozen-Score Controls: Conditional Gains and Limits for Mean Inference.\n\n**Central question:** With labels, unlabeled observations, and a scalar predictor fixed within each repeated-sampling comparison, when does training-only cross-fitted score calibration shorten mean confidence intervals relative to label-only inference, PPI, and PPI++, and what does the evidence establish about coverage?\n\n**Central thesis:** The frozen risk-adaptive control method yields narrower mean intervals than PPI and PPI++ on the evaluated empirical gene-expression source, but is wider than PPI++ in all six synthetic or perturbed-score conditions. Observed coverage and offset-invariant studentization support a conditional efficiency study, not universal narrowing or a finite-sample validity certificate.\n\nThis is one shared thesis with condition-specific evidence, not separate dataset papers. The contribution is an implemented, carefully delimited mean-inference construction, explanatory algebra, and a matched assessment of gains and limitations. It is not a claim of a new generally optimal interval or an identified causal mechanism for the measured gain.\n\n## Evidence and qualification audit\n\nRead `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently. `downstream_study` is absent. The authoritative sources are:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: completed.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: qualified.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: true.\n- `final.evidence.episodes`: 400 final repeats, seven conditions, paired endpoints for the frozen method and all three references.\n- `final.metric.empirical_coverage`: 0.9610714285714286; `final.metric.minimum_case_coverage`: 0.95; nominal coverage 0.95; failure fraction 0.\n- `final.metric.mean_width_ratio`: 1.2106794607646876, a capped mean of episode ratios, not a ratio of mean widths.\n\nThere is no failed final qualification in the current evidence. Qualification is nevertheless not a coverage theorem. The local resource manifest and final evaluator explicitly fix the conservative scientific-validity field at zero because arbitrary theoretical assumptions cannot be certified. That flag is neither a diagnosed coverage/adaptivity failure nor a positive certificate. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; prefer evaluator-observed endpoints and evaluator-produced summaries.\n\nIf later authoritative evidence changes qualification to failed, preserve that outcome prominently in writing, review, audit, and revision. Never infer an undisclosed gate cause or turn publication acceptance into scientific qualification.\n\nThe selected search record directly identifies `results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1`; its `src/program.py` and `src/METHOD.md` were inspected. The final record supplies a frozen tree hash, but this stage did not independently recompute it against the local source. Keep that verification distinction. `ablation_studies` is empty and its evidence directory is missing.\n\n## Framing alternatives and staged-input impact\n\n**Prior-story status: rejected by default, not an actual manuscript rejection.** No edit context, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is staged. There is no prior manuscript to preserve or broaden.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve a prospective nonlinear saturation/certificate success story | Reject: oracle identities are available, but the reported interval is not the internal certificate, and nonlinear synthetic data do not show a width benefit against PPI++. |\n| Broaden to a shared family including smooth covariance stacking | Reject as the primary thesis: stacking has exploratory search evidence, not the frozen method'\"'s final assessment; no controlled component ablations identify a common improvement mechanism. |\n| Recenter on the most recent searched configuration | Reject: recency is weaker evidence than the selected method's completed final repeats. |\n| Conditional efficiency and validity boundaries of the frozen risk-adaptive method | Select: supported by matched final endpoints, all three references, uncertainty, information controls, and explicit source-level limitations. |\n\nThe final evidence changes the private source-only perspective into a measured conditional-results story. No inherited title or figure plan is retained by inertia. Constant/permuted scores support the shared question without becoming independent natural datasets. This structure transfers to other tasks by selecting the frozen method, retaining valid matched references, and organizing conditions by the boundaries they test rather than by execution order.\n\n## Narrow method set\n\n1. **Baseline: label-only mean intervals.** Clopper-Pearson for binary outcomes; Student-t for real outcomes. The latter is not distribution-free exact for the empirical real source.\n2. **Baselines: PPI and PPI++.** Both are matched implementations from \"'`ppi-python==0.2.3`; distinguish coefficient one from the optimized coefficient. Keep the original comparator and the optimized reference, with their asymptotic and rare-event caveats.\n3. **Proposed: risk-adaptive cross-fitted frozen-score controls.** Nested training-only zero/affine/spline selection, finite-unlabeled-sample multiplier, held-out correction, and offset-invariant within-fold/shared-pool studentization. Explain the actual t-based return path, clipping, and label-only fallbacks.\n4. **Supporting conditions: constant and permuted scores.** These perturb the nonlinear records, complementing the uninformative condition. They are information controls, not isolated ablations of nonlinear calibration or studentization.\n\nExclude smooth stacking and pooled-residual variants from the main method set. Their existing search evidence may appear in an explicitly exploratory supplementary comparison, not as final or causal ablations. Do not invent a performance comparison to an unavailable original incumbent. No `study.*` sources exist; do not fabricate downstream-study selection facts, differences, or scored/unscored counts.\n\n## Results that carry the story\n\nUse every final condition: gene expression, linear, nonlinear, rare outcomes, uninformative, constant-score nonlinear, and permuted-score nonlinear. Group the first four as predictive conditions and the last three as information controls. All required comparison figures use this same partition.\n\n**Empirical-source gain.** In the stored gene-expression summary, mean widths are 0.4488074070562893 for the frozen method, 0.4759006085515874 for PPI++, 0.4727476605089987 for PPI, and 1.5263806745747615 for label-only inference. The paired width-difference interval versus PPI++ is [-0.02969064263718976, -0.02435572792761422]. Candidate coverage is 0.9575 with stored simultaneous interval [0.9163118126035984, 0.9824159148164467]. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, especially the first episode'\"'s \"'`solution.summary.gene_expression` and the paired endpoints throughout the episodes.\n\n**Boundary of benefit.** All six other conditions have positive paired width-difference intervals versus PPI++. Preserve these results in the main paper, including the explicitly nonlinear condition. Gains against label-only inference or PPI do not imply gains against PPI++.\n\n**Coverage uncertainty.** Candidate condition coverage spans 0.95 to 0.98, but its lower uncertainty bounds do not establish coverage at or above nominal. Plot all four methods'\"' stored Clopper-Pearson intervals and the 0.95 reference. These intervals use a joint 95% Bonferroni family over seven conditions and four methods.\n\n**Rare-outcome caveat.** PPI++ observed coverage is 0.92; its interval [0.868958106205729, 0.9562534828761202] includes nominal coverage. Five of its 400 intervals have zero width, counted directly from paired endpoints. Retain its measurements, but do not equate its narrower intervals with a valid practical improvement or claim a statistically established coverage failure. This caveat belongs in the body, not only in the supplement.\n\n**Width estimand audit.** The final evaluator caps each candidate/PPI++ width ratio at 100 and assigns 100 when only the baseline width is zero. The recorded aggregate is secondary. Main comparisons use absolute paired mean differences and ratios of mean widths with their matching stored bootstrap intervals. Do not drop zero-width episodes or silently interchange these quantities. The width intervals use 512 paired bootstrap resamples, recorded seed 79231, and multiplicity-adjusted percentile tails. Explain finite-bootstrap tail resolution; do not claim exact simultaneous bootstrap coverage.\n\n**Mechanism without causal overreach.** Explain deterministic-control finite-N headroom and offset cancellation, but do not attribute the measured gain to nonlinear fitting, selection, or studentization individually. No final component ablations exist. Critical values, clipping, and fallbacks also differ between methods.\n\n## Scope and method explanation\n\nThe estimand is a mean. Resource fixing holds within each matched replicate; coverage concerns repeated sampling, not conditioning on every realized input. The real benchmark resamples normalized scalar outcome/prediction records with replacement and targets that empirical source's mean. It does not validate external biological-population inference, a new backbone, or promoter-sequence disjointness.\n\nDescribe the weighted held-out residual mean plus the shared-unlabeled control mean. Compute unlabeled variance after combining fold controls to retain covariance. Show why fold-specific additive constants cancel from both the point estimate and the proposed studentizer. This does not show that pooled residual studentization empirically failed.\n\nSeparate deterministic-control identities from learned-fold asymptotics: overlapping training sets do not permit an exact conditional independence argument. State the source note's stability and moment conditions; the heuristic inner-fold risk comparison does not establish them or constitute a calibrated significance test. The internal bounded certificate is computed but not returned. A label-only exact binomial fallback does not certify a data-dependent combined rule.\n\n## Figure specifications and layout\n\nInspected \"'`.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex` and `paper.sty`: the supplied format is **single-column**, with **5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height**. Here column width equals full text width; a hypothetical half-width two-column figure would be wrong. Historical style-header text does not certify official ICLR 2026 provenance. All three figures use full text width, with no progression axis.\n\n### Figure 1: `fixed_resource_overview` — required\n\n- **Type/generator:** conceptual diagram; `generate-concept-figure`, the only figure using that workflow.\n- **Content:** fixed inputs and outer-training-only zero/affine/spline selection in one panel; held-out correction, shared-pool aggregation, and the returned interval in the other. Mark the separation between returned inference and empirical assessment, not a certified-success arrow.\n- **Budgets:** at most two panels, six functional groups per panel, two visual series; 9–10pt text.\n- **Footprint:** 1.0 text-width fraction, approximately 5.5 by 2.0 inches; near one quarter page with caption, on page 1 or 2.\n- **Justification:** the training boundary and shared-pool combination are not immediately legible from result plots alone. Two compact conceptual panels provide glance value without reproducing a software execution diary.\n- **Execution constraint:** the downstream orchestrator owns rendering. Honor no paid APIs: deterministic draw.io rendering without optional paid image restyling; if orchestration cannot honor that constraint, report the blocker rather than silently paying or changing ownership.\n\n### Figure 2: `paired_width_comparison` — required\n\n- **Type/generator:** paired-bootstrap forest plot; matplotlib.\n- **Metric:** candidate mean width divided by comparator mean width; three series for label-only, PPI, and PPI++ with matching stored ratio intervals. Record deterministic derivations from the stored means and endpoint sources.\n- **Grouping:** two vertically stacked panels: four predictive conditions, then three information controls. Common log-ratio axis; reference one. Do not imply that a width advantage establishes coverage.\n- **Budgets:** two panels, at most four conditions per panel, three series; minimum 8pt text, preferably 9pt.\n- **Footprint:** 1.0 text-width fraction, 5.5 inches wide by approximately 3.3–3.7 inches total height; about half a page with caption.\n- **Justification:** stacked full-width panels keep condition labels and three uncertainty series legible; shrinking two panels side by side would not.\n\n### Figure 3: `coverage_with_uncertainty` — required\n\n- **Type/generator:** binomial-interval forest plot; matplotlib.\n- **Metric:** empirical coverage with the stored simultaneous intervals; frozen method plus three references. One nominal line at 0.95.\n- **Grouping:** same two-panel vertical partition as Figure 2 and common axis covering every stored endpoint. No thresholded success colors, cropped lower bounds, or pass/fail interpretation when an interval spans nominal.\n- **Budgets:** two panels, four conditions per panel, four series; 8–9pt text.\n- **Footprint:** 1.0 text-width fraction, 5.5 inches wide by approximately 3.3–3.7 inches total height; about half a page with caption.\n- **Justification:** directly pairs coverage with the efficiency comparison and keeps rare-outcome uncertainty visible.\n\nRetain quantitative plotting source and export PDF plus 300-DPI PNG. LaTeX references PDF whenever available. Keep editable draw.io source and its vector export when available. Raster-only full-width output requires at least 2400 pixels across; column-width output requires at least 1200. A 5.5-inch raster at 300 DPI alone does not reach the full-width minimum: regenerate larger from source rather than upscale.\n\n## Body-page use\n\nTarget nine substantive ICLR body pages, excluding references and supplement. Planning allocations include floats, not just prose:\n\n| Material | Page budget |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title and abstract | 0.5 |\n| Introduction and early overview | 1.2 |\n| Related work | 0.6 |\n| Method | 1.6 |\n| Identities and validity boundaries | 1.1 |\n| Evaluation design | 0.7 |\n| Results and two quantitative figures | 2.1 |\n| Discussion and limitations | 0.9 |\n| Conclusion | 0.3 |\n\nIf the body instead finishes at eight pages, its last page must be at least 75% full. Use space for assumptions, comparator distinctions, uncertainty definitions, and substantive failure analysis, not filler. Add a compact full-width design table describing conditions, targets, pairing, and transformations. No new experiments are needed to fill the plan.\n\nSuggested sections: Introduction; Related Work; Mean Inference with Frozen Scores; Control Construction and Validity Boundaries; Evaluation Design; Conditional Width Gains and Coverage; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Preserve the standalone template'\"'s required core sections, using these as appropriate section/subsection names.\n\n## Supplementary Information after references\n\n**Purpose:** add reproducible technical and audit depth, not hide central non-benefits or coverage limitations. Use \"'`clearpage`, bibliography, `clearpage`, appendix, and a Supplementary Information section in the same manuscript. Route authoritative later appendix-ranked analyses there without retroactively editing this strategy.\n\n1. **Extended control construction and variance identities.** Expand selected `src/METHOD.md` and `src/program.py`: deterministic finite-N variance/headroom, offset cancellation, shared-pool covariance, nested fitting, signed multipliers, spline penalties, critical values, clipping, fallbacks, and assumptions. Source IDs: `result.best_correct_score`, `final.evidence.episodes`. Existing `test_controls.py` is source, not a passed test report.\n2. **Complete paired interval and coverage tables.** All conditions and methods, exact coverage counts, means, uncertainty, and missingness; retain principal comparisons in the body. Source IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.nominal_coverage`, `final.metric.failure_fraction`.\n3. **Rare outcomes and width-ratio definitions.** Identify zero-width episodes by seed; audit mean differences, ratios of means, and capped episode ratios from `task/evaluate_final.py`. Avoid undefined infinite averages and preserve baseline coverage uncertainty. Source IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.mean_width_ratio`, `final.metric.empirical_coverage`.\n4. **Frozen-source provenance and reproduction.** Use `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, `task/evaluate_final.py`, `task/requirements.txt`, selected code, and final manifests. Document 128 labels, 8192 unlabeled scores, seeds 1000–1399, pairing, normalized duplicate-group separation, empirical-source sampling, and hashes. Do not invent unrecovered synthetic generative equations from condition names: frozen arrays support replay. Map every final table/figure to its actual script and command when implemented; report only recorded runtimes. Independent NPZ redistribution rights remain unresolved; do not bundle data without permission. Source IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, `result.best_correct_score`.\n5. **Exploratory configuration comparisons and selection boundary — optional.** Existing 100-repeat search records can compare pooled-residual controls, offset-invariant risk-adaptive controls, and smooth stacking, with explicit adaptive-search caveats. No chronological ordering, causal-ablation claims, pooling with final repeats, unknown selection frequencies, or fabricated downstream-study counts. Omit if not scientifically useful. Source IDs: `result.top_program_01`, `result.top_program_02`, `result.top_program_03`, `result.best_correct_score`.\n6. **LLM Usage.** Separate required disclosure of documented ideation, editing, and framing roles, adding the actual writer'\"'s role later. Do not invent human verification, tests, or reference consultation. Source IDs: \"'`result.best_correct_score`, `final.evidence.episodes`.\n\n## Writing, reference, and anonymity safeguards\n\n- No isolated calibration ablations, parameter sweeps, oracle experiments, selection-frequency studies, extra real datasets, or predictor retraining are supported as completed final experiments.\n- Most episodes do not contain repeated summaries. Read the summary at `final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes[0].solution.summary` and paired endpoints from each episode'\"'s \"'`solution.cases`.\n- A key-only scan of the newly retrieved bibliography exposed unscreened recent candidates. No associated abstracts, pages, or full texts were read here; no prohibited work was identified. Quarantine those entries rather than trying to infer the forbidden paper'\"'s identity. Bibliography and provenance presence do not establish safe or actually consulted references.\n- Permitted exact PPI and Cross-PPI records are starting points. Resolve PPI++ only through permitted \"'`arXiv:2311.01453` and use other permitted starter works only as needed. Avoid citation-graph expansion, related-paper feeds, and unscreened modern searches. Stop and log location/extent if prohibited content is encountered. Do not invent citations or novelty claims.\n- Confirm official ICLR 2026 template provenance using venue-only sources before final submission. Remove the supplied template'\"'s named authors and final-copy author presentation; omit affiliations, acknowledgments, funding, and identifying links. Preserve the orchestrator-owned watermark. Do not invent a venue checklist requirement.\n- Internal qualification fields and selection identifiers in this memo/JSON are **private audit context**, not recommended visible paper metrics or labels. Do not expose pipeline terminology in reader-facing tables or plots.\n\n## Forbidden organization and wording\n\nAvoid headings: Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Experiment A / Experiment B; Iteration History; From Baseline to Winner; Universal Interval Shortening; Certified Coverage of the Returned Method.\n\nAvoid phrases: “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “winning iteration,” “state of the art,” “uniformly narrower,” “guaranteed nominal coverage for the returned intervals,” and “the nonlinear component caused the observed gain.” Also prohibit visible pipeline labels: \"'`correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`.\n\n## Stage boundary\n\nThis is a strategy, not a final paper. Do not create `paper.tex` or `claims_grounding.json`, render final figures, run concept generation, or invoke final-paper validation in this stage. Strategy/schema and evidence-consistency checks are appropriate. Missing citation consultation and template provenance are downstream completion requirements, not permission to fabricate either.\nEOF\npython -m json.tool paper_strategy.json >/dev/null'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
